    "available_at": "2026-10-23T14:44:14.902236+00:00",
    "body": "2026 Paris Motor Show • 2026 Paris Motor Show: Jeep Reveals Six-Model European Strategy for 2030, Renegade Is Set to Return\nAfter Nearly 7 Years, Tesla Confirms the Cybertruck Isn't Coming to Australia\n11 photos\nPhoto: Tesla / edited\nNot surprising anyone, Tesla has ended its Cybertruck plans for Australia and New Zealand. This development leaves reservation holders with their money back, plus a shameful pitch for completely different Tesla models. The one-year FSD subscription in the cancellation email applies only when a customer orders a new Model 3 or Model Y, and this complimentary subscription is anything but compensation for nearly 7 years of waiting.\nTesla will send a separate email asking reservation holders to confirm their refund details, then process the payment within 30 days of confirmation. As if almost 7 years of waiting wasn't enough. Reservations opened in 2019, when the Cybertruck was both all the rage and the most ridiculed production vehicle of all time. Australian customers had to put down AUD 150 deposits, and the reservation scheme curiously outlived the Australian order page.\nThe American electric vehicle automaker closed it back in 2022, and even more curiously, Tesla did not automatically return everybody's deposits. With this email, Tesla is finally giving those would-be customers a definitive answer. Why didn't the company end things in 2024? As it happens, a publicity tour briefly revived hopes of a right-hand-drive version for this part of the world.\nSomeone at Tesla even decided on shipping a Cybertruck to Australia, displaying it at public events and in showrooms around the country to (re)build hype. Showing the Cybertruck to potential customers isn't the same as preparing a right-hand-drive model with the definitive plan of selling the Cybertruck in Australia.\nThat's not all, though, because Teslas for Australia and New Zealand require fine-tuning suited for said countries. Developing this variant makes little financial sense, and Elon Musk argued that himself during the 2024 shareholder meeting. Worse still, the commercial picture has also changed since the Cybertruck's way too pompous unveiling.\nPhoto: Tesla Instead of selling 250,000 units per annum, with possibilities of higher volumes, the Cybertruck barely moved 21,000 units in the United States of America last year. In the first three quarters of 2026, fewer than 12,000 units were delivered. Given that American drivers aren't particularly convinced either, why should Tesla pour valuable R&D money into developing and certifying a low-volume Cybertruck version for Australia and New Zealand?\nThe Full Self-Driving (Supervised) offer in the email is best viewed as a slap in the face to the peeps who gave the Cybertruck a chance in the first place. With FSD retailing at AUD 149 per month, a full year would be AUD 1,788, which is not nearly enough for wasting one's time like this.\nWhile said offer may be useful to someone who already plans to buy the Model 3 or Y, it feels like rubbing salt in the wound of reservation holders who wanted the pickup and nothing else. Otherwise put, a coupon for a purchase they never intended to make rather than cash compensation.\nAs of this writing, the Cybertruck carries a starting price of USD 74,990 stateside, making it way pricier than a combustion half-ton pickup with four-wheel drive. Opting for the Premium grade means $84,990 from the outset, whereas $99,990 gets you the performance-oriented Cyberbeast.\nBoth the base Dual Motor All-Wheel Drive and the Premium All-Wheel Drive top 325 miles (523 kilometers) on a full charge. As for acceleration, Tesla says the Cyberbeast needs only 2.6 clicks to hit 60 mph (97 kph).\nVia // Sawyer Merritt on X\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more articles from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nTesla Cybertruck Tesla Australia New Zealand EV\nAbout the author: Mircea Panait\nAfter a 1:43 scale model of a Ferrari 250 GTO sparked Mircea's interest for cars when he was a kid, an early internship at Top Gear sealed his career path. He's most interested in muscle cars and American trucks, but he takes a passing interest in quirky kei cars as well. Full profile\nUP NEXT New York City Has a Taste for Luxury, and the Name on Everyone's Lips Is the 2026 Lexus RX",
